@page "/your-plan"
<PageTitle>Your plan · Tangent Atlas</PageTitle>

<div class="ta-container ta-page">
    <h1 class="ta-visually-hidden">Your plan</h1>   @* The menu already names the page; the heading is kept for screen readers and search. *@
    <p class="ta-page-idea">What do you own?</p>
    <p class="ta-page-sub">A few hundred shares of one company. A few hundred of another. A big chunk in index funds. Cash spread out between three banks and a brokerage firm. But why, exactly, do you own this combination?</p>
    <p class="ta-page-statement">Do you have a plan?</p>

    <h2 class="ta-page-statement">No? Then build one: your own personal index.</h2>
    <p class="ta-page-sub">Everyone who owns an S&amp;P 500 fund buys the same risk. But the risk appetite of someone in their 30s isn’t the same as someone in their 60s. Select how many companies you want to own and how much you want to keep in cash. Fewer companies means more risk; more cash means less risk. Once you’ve chosen, your index is rebalanced using the same method as the big index funds.</p>
    <p class="ta-page-sub">Do you want all the benefits of the S&amp;P 500 without owning every company in it? Maybe your employer has restrictions, maybe you don’t like the brand. Maybe you believe in ESG, or maybe your faith drives your decisions. Leave out the companies you can’t own or don’t want. Enjoy your dividends knowing they reflect your values.</p>

    <p class="ta-page-sub">Here’s what that looks like for Ken.</p>

    <div class="ta-holdings">
        <div class="ta-holdings-name">Ken today</div>
        <div class="ta-holdings-bar" role="img" aria-label="Ken today: JPM $60,000, DIS $40,000, SPY $200,000, QQQ $120,000, cash $80,000">
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-c1" style="width: 12%"><b>JPM</b>$60k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-c2" style="width: 8%"><b>DIS</b>$40k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-c3" style="width: 40%"><b>SPY</b>$200k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-c4" style="width: 24%"><b>QQQ</b>$120k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-cash" style="width: 16%"><b>Cash</b>$80k</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ta-plan-stats">
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Annual return</span><span class="ta-plan-value">10.4%</span></div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Volatility</span><span class="ta-plan-value">15.5%</span></div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Sharpe ratio</span><span class="ta-plan-value">0.48</span></div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Worst drop</span><span class="ta-plan-value">−26%</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="ta-page-sub">Ken has a few companies he doesn’t want to own. His paycheck already depends on Google, and he doesn’t want his retirement to depend on it too. He believes in Tesla, but he thinks it’s overvalued, and he hasn’t used social media in years. So GOOGL, GOOG, TSLA and META stay out of his index.</p>
    <p class="ta-page-sub">Now suppose Ken had built his own personal index from the top 34 companies in the S&amp;P 500. Remove his four tickers, put more of his money in cash, and here’s how it would have performed.</p>

    <div class="ta-holdings">
        <div class="ta-holdings-name">The Ken 30</div>
        <div class="ta-holdings-bar" role="img" aria-label="The Ken 30: NVDA $58,000, AAPL $53,000, MSFT $40,000, 27 more companies $199,000, cash $150,000">
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-c1" style="width: 11.6%"><b>NVDA</b>$58k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-c3" style="width: 10.6%"><b>AAPL</b>$53k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-c2" style="width: 8%"><b>MSFT</b>$40k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-rest" style="width: 39.8%"><b>27 more</b>$199k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-cash" style="width: 30%"><b>Cash</b>$150k</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ta-plan-stats ta-plan-stats-better">
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Annual return</span><span class="ta-plan-value">11.2%</span></div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Volatility</span><span class="ta-plan-value">13.3%</span></div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Sharpe ratio</span><span class="ta-plan-value">0.59</span></div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Worst drop</span><span class="ta-plan-value">−18%</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="ta-page-statement">More return. Less risk. More cash.</p>
    <p class="ta-page-sub">By not having a plan, Ken earns less, takes more risk, falls further in a bad year, and has less cash for emergencies. His Sharpe ratio tells the story in one number: it measures how much you’re paid for the risk you take; the higher, the better. With a plan, Ken’s ratio rises from 0.48 to 0.59.</p>
    <p class="ta-page-sub">We don’t promise the next five years will look like the last five. A plan isn’t a forecast, but having one beats not having one.</p>

    <div class="ta-page-final">
        <p class="ta-page-close">Your index. Your shares. Your plan.</p>
        <AuthorizeView>
            <NotAuthorized>
                <a href="/signup" class="ta-btn ta-btn-primary ta-btn-large">Sign up</a>
            </NotAuthorized>
        </AuthorizeView>
    </div>
</div>

<section class="ta-band ta-fineprint">
    <div class="ta-container">
        <p class="ta-figures"><strong>About these figures.</strong> These are backtested results from October 22, 2021 to October 23, 2026: they represent a hypothetical illustration, not a prediction or a recommendation. The Ken 30 holds the 30 largest S&amp;P 500 companies other than the four he leaves out, reweighted by size each quarter, with 30% in cash. Cash earns nothing in either portfolio. Sharpe ratios use the return on short-term Treasury bills as the risk-free rate. Returns include dividends but not taxes or trading costs.</p>
    </div>
</section>
